"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[41432],{141432:n=>{n.exports="# How to data preview analysis\n\n## Example: Data Quality Analysis\n\nFor understanding overall data quality before configuring transformations:\n\n**Approach:**\n\n1. Analyze the bronze table to get quality metrics for each column\n2. Review null rates, cardinality, and value distributions\n3. Identify columns with issues (high null rates, inconsistent formats)\n4. Recommend appropriate transformations\n\n**Sample Response:**\n\n```\n**Data Quality Assessment**\n\nAnalyzed 1,000 sample records from the bronze table. Here's what I found:\n\n**Good Quality Columns:**\n✓ event_id - 100% populated, high cardinality (likely unique)\n✓ timestamp - 100% populated, consistent ISO format\n✓ user_id - 99.8% populated\n\n**Issues Found:**\n⚠ source_ip - 15% null values (consider default value or filter)\n⚠ location.country - Inconsistent formats detected ('US', 'USA', 'United States')\n⚠ metadata - Low cardinality (only 3 distinct values) - might indicate data issues\n✗ legacy_field - 100% null (recommend omitting from silver table)\n\n**Recommendations:**\n1. Add a filter to exclude records with null source_ip, or use COALESCE for default\n2. Normalize country codes using a CASE expression\n3. Consider omitting legacy_field using omitFields in presetOverrides\n\nWould you like me to configure any of these transformations?\n```\n\n## Example: Null Value Analysis\n\nFor identifying columns with missing data:\n\n**Sample Response:**\n\n```\nI analyzed null values across all columns. Here's the breakdown:\n\n**Completely Null (100%):**\n- legacy_field: 1,000/1,000 records are null\n- deprecated_column: 1,000/1,000 records are null\n\n**High Null Rate (>50%):**\n- optional_metadata: 72% null (720/1,000 records)\n- secondary_ip: 65% null (650/1,000 records)\n\n**Moderate Null Rate (20-50%):**\n- location.region: 35% null (350/1,000 records)\n\n**Recommendations:**\n- Omit completely null columns from silver table\n- For high null rate columns, decide if you want to:\n  - Filter out null records\n  - Use COALESCE to provide default values\n  - Keep as-is if nulls are expected\n\nWould you like me to configure these changes?\n```\n\n## Example: Value Distribution Analysis\n\nFor understanding the distribution of values in a specific column:\n\n**Sample Response:**\n\n```\n**Value Distribution for 'event_type'**\n\nDistinct values: 8\nNull rate: 0%\n\nTop values:\n| Value          | Count | Percentage |\n|----------------|-------|------------|\n| LOGIN          | 450   | 45%        |\n| LOGOUT         | 280   | 28%        |\n| PASSWORD_RESET | 120   | 12%        |\n| MFA_CHALLENGE  | 80    | 8%         |\n| ACCOUNT_LOCK   | 40    | 4%         |\n| Other (3 more) | 30    | 3%         |\n\n**Observations:**\n- Low cardinality (8 values) - good candidate for filtering or splitting\n- LOGIN events dominate (45%) - consider if this distribution is expected\n- No null values\n\nWould you like to:\n1. Create separate silver tables for different event types?\n2. Filter to specific event types only?\n```\n\n## Example: Error Diagnosis\n\nFor understanding why a silver preview failed:\n\n**Sample Response:**\n\n```\n**Preview Error Analysis**\n\nThe silver preview failed with the following error:\n\n> AnalysisException: Column 'user_name' does not exist.\n> Available columns: [user_id, user_email, timestamp, event_type]\n\n**Problem:** Your silver configuration references a column 'user_name' that doesn't exist in the bronze table.\n\n**Likely Cause:**\n- Typo in the field spec `from` attribute\n- The column was renamed or removed from the source data\n- The column exists in a JSON field that hasn't been extracted\n\n**How to Fix:**\n1. Check your field specs for references to 'user_name'\n2. Use 'user_id' or 'user_email' instead if that's what you meant\n3. If the value is in a JSON column, inspect the JSON shape and follow the page-level JSON extraction guardrail before recommending a transformation.\n\nWould you like me to:\n1. Show you the bronze schema to find the correct column name?\n2. Update the field spec to use an existing column?\n```\n\n## Common Error Types\n\n### SQL Syntax Errors\n\n**Example Error:**\n\n```\nParseException: Syntax error at 'SELEC': expected 'SELECT', 'FROM', ...\n```\n\n**Diagnosis:**\n\n- Typo in SQL expression (e.g., 'SELEC' instead of 'SELECT')\n- Missing quotes around string literals\n- Incorrect function syntax\n\n**Common Fixes:**\n\n- Check spelling of SQL keywords\n- Ensure string literals are quoted: `'value'` not `value`\n- Verify function names and argument counts\n\n### Schema Mismatch Errors\n\n**Example Error:**\n\n```\nAnalysisException: Column 'xyz' does not exist. Available columns: [a, b, c]\n```\n\n**Diagnosis:**\n\n- Field spec references a non-existent column\n- Column was renamed in source data\n- Column is nested in JSON and not yet extracted\n\n**Common Fixes:**\n\n- Analyze bronze schema to see available columns\n- Check for typos in column names\n- Inspect JSON columns first, then follow the page-level JSON extraction guardrail before recommending a transformation.\n\n### Type Casting Errors\n\n**Example Error:**\n\n```\nNumberFormatException: For input string: \"abc\"\n```\n\n**Diagnosis:**\n\n- Attempting to cast a string to a number when the value isn't numeric\n- Data contains unexpected formats\n\n**Common Fixes:**\n\n- Add a filter to exclude invalid values\n- Use TRY_CAST instead of CAST for graceful handling\n- Clean the data with REGEXP_REPLACE before casting\n\n### Join Errors\n\n**Example Error:**\n\n```\nAnalysisException: Cannot resolve 'table.column' given input columns\n```\n\n**Diagnosis:**\n\n- Join table alias is incorrect\n- Join column doesn't exist in the referenced table\n- Table is not included in bronzeTables configuration\n\n**Common Fixes:**\n\n- Verify table alias matches bronzeTables configuration\n- Check column names in the join table\n- Ensure the table is added to bronzeTables\n\n### Filter Expression Errors\n\n**Example Error:**\n\n```\nAnalysisException: Filter expression must return boolean, got STRING\n```\n\n**Diagnosis:**\n\n- Filter expression returns a string instead of boolean\n- Missing comparison operator\n\n**Common Fixes:**\n\n- Add comparison: `status = 'active'` not just `status`\n- Use proper boolean expressions: `column IS NOT NULL`\n\n## Quality Metrics Explained\n\n### Null Rate\n\n- **0%**: Column is fully populated - good\n- **1-20%**: Some missing values - may be acceptable\n- **20-50%**: Significant missing data - consider handling\n- **50-99%**: Mostly empty - evaluate if column is needed\n- **100%**: Completely empty - recommend omitting\n\n### Cardinality (Distinct Count)\n\n- **1**: Single value - might be a constant or data issue\n- **2-10**: Low cardinality - good for filtering/grouping\n- **10-100**: Moderate cardinality - typical for categorical data\n- **100+**: High cardinality - likely an identifier or free-text\n- **Equal to row count**: Likely a unique identifier\n\n### Type Consistency Issues\n\n- **Multiple date formats**: Normalize with a CASE expression\n- **Inconsistent casing**: Use UPPER() or LOWER() to normalize\n- **Invalid numbers**: Use TRY_CAST or filter invalid values\n- **Whitespace issues**: Use TRIM() to clean values\n\n## Transformation Recommendations by Issue\n\n| Issue                 | Recommended Transformation                                                   |\n| --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| 100% null column      | `omitFields` or `unreferencedColumns.omitColumns`                            |\n| High null rate        | `filter` to exclude nulls, or `COALESCE` for default                         |\n| Inconsistent formats  | `CASE` expression or `REGEXP_REPLACE`                                        |\n| Type casting needed   | `CAST` or `TRY_CAST` in field `expr`                                         |\n| JSON needs extraction | Inspect the JSON shape, then follow the page-level JSON extraction guardrail |\n| Column rename         | `from` attribute in field spec                                               |\n\n## Best Practices\n\n### Start with data quality analysis\n\nBefore configuring any transformations, analyze the bronze data to understand:\n\n- Which columns have quality issues\n- What transformations are actually needed\n- Whether the data matches expectations\n\n### Focus on actionable insights\n\nWhen presenting data quality findings:\n\n- Highlight issues that need attention\n- Provide specific recommendations\n- Offer to implement the fixes\n\n### Explain errors in plain language\n\nWhen diagnosing preview errors:\n\n- Translate technical error messages\n- Explain the likely cause\n- Provide specific steps to fix\n\n## Common Mistakes to Avoid\n\n### ❌ Recommending transformations without analyzing data\n\n**Problem:** Suggesting transformations without first understanding data quality.\n\n**Impact:** May recommend unnecessary changes or miss critical issues.\n\n**Solution:** Always analyze data quality first to make informed recommendations.\n\n### ❌ Showing raw technical metrics without interpretation\n\n**Problem:** Displaying null rates and cardinality without explaining what they mean.\n\n**Impact:** Users don't understand the implications or what actions to take.\n\n**Solution:** Interpret metrics and provide actionable recommendations.\n\n### ❌ Ignoring error context when diagnosing failures\n\n**Problem:** Just reporting the error without analyzing the cause.\n\n**Impact:** Users can't fix the issue without understanding what went wrong.\n\n**Solution:** Parse error messages, identify the root cause, and suggest specific fixes.\n"}}]);
